[{q:"Is this a finished product?",a:"SmarterPlaylists is a fully released product. Your programs and playlists are preserved across updates."},{q:"What is SmarterPlaylists?",a:"SmarterPlaylists lets you build complex Spotify playlists by wiring together components in a visual graph editor. You can mix, filter, sort, and combine tracks from any Spotify source — playlists, saved tracks, recommendations, and more."},{q:"How do I create a program?",a:`Click "New Program" on the Programs page, or go to the Editor tab. Click a component in the palette on the left to add it to the canvas, then connect nodes by dragging from one node's output to another's input. The "main" node (marked with a star) determines what tracks are output when you run the program.`},{q:"What happens when I click Run?",a:"Running a program executes the graph and saves the output tracks to a Spotify playlist named after your program. The first time you run, a new playlist is created. On subsequent runs, the same playlist is updated with the new tracks."},{q:"What does Preview do?",a:"Preview runs the program and shows you the output tracks, but does not save them to a Spotify playlist. Use it to test and debug your program without modifying any playlists."},{q:"What keyboard shortcuts are available in the editor?",a:"",bullets:["Double-click a node to edit its parameters","Space — set the selected node as main output","Delete / Backspace — remove selected nodes",'Shift+Delete / Shift+Backspace — "splice" a node out of a chain, reconnecting its upstream sources to its downstream targets',"Cmd+C / Ctrl+C — copy selected nodes","Cmd+V / Ctrl+V — paste copied nodes","Cmd+Click — add individual nodes to a selection","Drag on canvas — draw a selection rectangle"]},{q:"What do the Snap and Layout buttons do?",a:`Click "Snap" in the top-right corner of the editor to toggle grid snapping — nodes will snap to a 20-pixel grid as you drag them. Click "Layout" to automatically arrange all nodes in a clean top-to-bottom layout based on how they're connected. An "Undo" button appears after layout so you can revert to the previous positions.`},{q:"Can I copy and paste nodes?",a:"Yes! Select one or more nodes, press Cmd+C (Mac) or Ctrl+C (Windows/Linux) to copy, then Cmd+V or Ctrl+V to paste. Copied nodes appear offset from the originals, and any edges between copied nodes are preserved — so you can duplicate entire sub-graphs."},{q:"Can I drag and drop from Spotify?",a:"Yes! You can drag playlists, albums, artists, or tracks directly from the Spotify desktop app onto the editor canvas. A component is automatically created with the right type and pre-filled with the name. You can also drag multiple tracks at once — they'll be combined into a single Track URIs node."},{q:"Can I drag components from the palette?",a:"Yes! Instead of clicking to place components, you can drag them from the palette directly onto the canvas. Even better, you can drag a component onto an existing node or edge to automatically wire it up:",bullets:["Drop on a node — connects the new component after that node. If the node already has a downstream connection, the new component is spliced in between.","Drop on an edge — inserts the new component into that connection, splitting it in two.","Edges animate and nodes glow blue when you hover over a valid drop target.","Inserted components automatically align with the upstream node."]},{q:"What are the colored input ports on nodes?",a:"Each component defines its own input ports. The color indicates the type of input: check the port description (shown on hover) for what each one expects. Common patterns include green for primary track input, red for track exclusion lists, and orange for artist exclusion — but the meaning depends on the specific component."},{q:"What does the star on a node mean?",a:`The star marks the "main" node — the component whose output becomes the program's final output. Select a node and press Space to set it as main.`},{q:"How do I schedule a program?",a:"On the Programs page, click the Schedule column for any program to set up automatic runs. You can choose hourly, daily, or weekly intervals. Scheduled programs run up to 100 times before stopping. If a program fails 3 times in a row, the schedule is automatically paused."},{q:"How can I organize my programs?",a:'You can tag programs with labels like "daily", "party", or "in progress" to keep things organized. Add tags from the Program Settings dialog in the editor, or click the tag icon in the Actions column on the Programs page for quick tagging. Tags appear as filter chips above the programs table — click one or more to filter your list. You can also type comma-separated tags to add several at once, and the editor will autocomplete from your existing tags.'},{q:'Do I need a "Save to Playlist" component at the end of my program?',a:"No! This is the most common source of confusion. Every program automatically saves its output to a Spotify playlist named after your program — no save component needed. If you add a PlaylistSave at the end, you'll get two playlists each time you run (one automatic, one from the component), which is usually not what you want. PlaylistSave and PlaylistSaveToNew exist for the rare case where you want to save a side branch of your program to a separate playlist — for example, keeping a running log of tracks that passed through a particular stage. They act as pass-throughs with a save side effect.",link:{text:"Read more on the blog",href:"https://musicmachinery.com/2026/03/07/you-dont-need-a-save-to-playlist-component/"}},{id:"description-variables",q:"What variables can I use in playlist descriptions?",a:"The Save to Playlist and Save to New Playlist components have a description field that gets written to the Spotify playlist. You can include variables that are resolved each time the program runs:",bullets:["${DATE} — current date (YYYY-MM-DD) in your local timezone","${TIME} — current time (HH:MM) in your local timezone","${PROGRAM_NAME} — name of the program","${PROGRAM_DESCRIPTION} — the program's description (set on the Programs page)",'${AUTO_PROGRAM_DESCRIPTION} — auto-generated summary of the program\'s dataflow (e.g. "Discover Weekly, filtered by energy, sorted by tempo")',"${PLAYLIST_NAME} — name of the playlist","${NUM_TRACKS} — number of tracks in output","${NUM_ARTISTS} — number of unique artists","${USER} — your Spotify display name",'${DAY-OF-WEEK} — e.g. "Monday"','${UPDATE_SCHEDULE} — schedule frequency (e.g. "daily", "weekly", or "not scheduled")','${DURATION} — total playlist duration (e.g. "2h 34m")','You can mix variables with your own text: "Updated ${DATE} — ${NUM_TRACKS} tracks, ${DURATION}"',"Spotify limits descriptions to 300 characters. Descriptions are truncated if they exceed this."]},{q:"Can I work with artists and albums, not just tracks?",a:"Yes! SmarterPlaylists supports three entity types: tracks, artists, and albums. You can source artists (My Top Artists, My Artist Library, Artist, Artist URIs) or albums (My Album Library, Album Entity, Album URIs, Label Albums) and flow them through the graph. Use adapters to convert between types: To Tracks converts artists or albums into their tracks, To Artists extracts artists from tracks or albums, To Albums extracts albums from tracks or fetches an artist's discography, and Similar Artists finds related artists. Many components (filters, sorters, selectors) are generic and work on any entity type — the attribute dropdown adapts automatically."},{q:"What are the violet and teal dashed lines in the editor?",a:"Connections in the editor are color-coded by entity type. Solid green lines carry tracks (the default). Dashed violet lines carry artist entities, and dashed teal lines carry album entities. Source and adapter node headers are also colored to match their output type. The editor prevents invalid connections — you can't connect an artist source to a track-only filter."},{q:"What are adapters?",a:`Adapters convert between entity types. "To Tracks" takes artists or albums and produces their tracks (with options like most popular, newest, album order, etc.). "To Artists" extracts unique artists from tracks or albums. "To Albums" extracts albums from tracks or fetches an artist's discography. "Similar Artists" takes artists and finds related artists via Spotify radio. The selection options adapt based on the input type — for example, To Tracks shows different modes for artist input vs. album input.`},{q:"Can I filter by audio features?",a:"Yes! Use the Attribute Range Filter to filter tracks by danceability, energy, valence, tempo, acousticness, instrumentalness, and many other audio features. You can also filter by popularity, release date, and track duration. When connected to artist or album streams, the Range Filter adapts its dropdown to show attributes relevant to that entity type."},{q:"What is the Multi-Objective Sequencer?",a:"The Multi-Objective Sequencer (MOS) is an advanced ordering component that builds a playlist track by track using multiple weighted goals. Unlike a simple sort, the MOS can optimize for many things at once — for example, maximize energy while spreading out artists and keeping tempo in a range. You configure objectives (like maximize, minimize, variety, min-separation) and the MOS picks the best next track at each step.",link:{text:"Read the full MOS reference guide",href:"/docs/mos-reference"}},{id:"track-attributes",q:"What track attributes are available?",a:"Every track flowing through a program carries these attributes. You can use them in filters (Attribute Range Filter, Text Filter, Genre Filter), sorters, and conditional components.",table:{headers:["Attribute","Type","Range","Description"],rows:[["acousticness","number","0.0–1.0","Confidence that the track is acoustic. 1.0 = high confidence it's acoustic."],["album","text","","The album name."],["album_popularity","number","0–100","The album's popularity score on Spotify."],["album_release_date","date","","The album release date (YYYY-MM-DD). Use this to filter by era or find recent releases."],["artist","text","","The primary artist name."],["artist_area","text","","The artist's area from MusicBrainz — may be a city, region, or country name."],["artist_country","text","","The artist's country of origin as an ISO code (e.g. US, GB, JP, DE)."],["artist_end_year","number","","The year the artist ended activity. Empty if still active."],["artist_followers","number","0+","How many Spotify users follow the primary artist."],["artist_gender","text","","Gender/vocal classification from MusicBrainz: male, female, mixed, instrumental, other, or unknown."],["artist_popularity","number","0–100","The primary artist's popularity score on Spotify, based on the popularity of all their tracks."],["artist_start_year","number","","The year the artist began activity (from MusicBrainz)."],["artist_type","text","","The type of artist from MusicBrainz: person, group, orchestra, choir, character, or other."],["camelot","text","e.g. 8B, 5A","The Camelot wheel code for DJ-friendly key matching. Tracks with adjacent Camelot codes mix well together."],["camelot_num","number","1–24","Numeric Camelot wheel position. Adjacent numbers (including wrap-around) indicate compatible keys for mixing."],["danceability","number","0.0–1.0","How suitable a track is for dancing, based on tempo, rhythm stability, beat strength, and regularity."],["disc_number","number","1+","The disc number (for multi-disc albums)."],["duration_ms","number","0+","Track length in milliseconds. For reference, 3 minutes = 180000 ms."],["energy","number","0.0–1.0","Perceptual intensity and activity. Death metal scores high; a Bach prelude scores low."],["explicit","boolean","true / false","Whether Spotify has marked the track as explicit."],["instrumentalness","number","0.0–1.0",'Predicts whether a track has no vocals. Values above 0.5 are likely instrumental. "Ooh" and "aah" sounds are treated as instrumental.'],["is_active","boolean","true / false","Whether the artist is still active (has no end date in MusicBrainz)."],["key","number","0–11","The musical key using pitch class notation: 0 = C, 1 = C♯/D♭, 2 = D, 3 = D♯/E♭, 4 = E, 5 = F, 6 = F♯/G♭, 7 = G, 8 = G♯/A♭, 9 = A, 10 = A♯/B♭, 11 = B."],["label","text","","The record label that released the album."],["liveness","number","0.0–1.0","Probability the track was performed live. Values above 0.8 strongly suggest a live recording."],["loudness","number","–60 to 5 dB","Average loudness in decibels. Typical tracks range from −30 to 0 dB. Useful for normalizing volume across a playlist."],["mb_genres","text list","",'Genre tags from MusicBrainz for the primary artist (e.g. "rock", "electronic", "jazz"). Used by the Genre Filter component.'],["mo_score","number","0.0–1.0","The Multi-Objective Sequencer score for this track (0 = best fit). Only present on tracks output by the MOS component."],["mode","number","0 or 1","The modality of the track: 0 = minor key, 1 = major key."],["name","text","","The track title."],["popularity","number","0–100","Spotify's popularity score. Higher means more recent streams. A track with 0 has almost no plays; 100 is one of the most-played tracks on Spotify right now."],["release_date","date","","The track release date (YYYY-MM-DD), from the album."],["release_decade","number","1900–2030","The decade the track was released (e.g. 1970, 1980, 1990). Derived from release_year. Useful for filtering or grouping by era."],["release_year","number","1900–2030","The year the track was released (derived from release_date). Useful for filtering or sorting by era."],["speechiness","number","0.0–1.0","Detects spoken words. > 0.66 is probably all speech (podcast, poetry). 0.33–0.66 may be rap or mixed. < 0.33 is most likely music."],["tempo","number","0–500 BPM","The estimated tempo in beats per minute. Most pop music falls between 90–150 BPM."],["time_signature","number","3–7","Estimated time signature. 4 means 4/4 time, 3 means 3/4 (waltz), etc."],["track_number","number","1+","The track's position on its album (e.g. 1 for the opening track)."],["uri","text","","The Spotify track URI (e.g. spotify:track:...). Useful with Max Match to deduplicate tracks."],["valence","number","0.0–1.0","Musical positiveness. High valence sounds happy/cheerful; low valence sounds sad/angry."]]}},{id:"artist-attributes",q:"What artist attributes are available?",a:"When you use artist sources (My Top Artists, My Artist Library, Artist, Artist URIs) or the To Artists adapter, each artist entity carries these attributes. Use them in the Range Filter, Match Filter, or Sort components.",table:{headers:["Attribute","Type","Range","Description"],rows:[["name","text","","The artist name."],["popularity","number","0–100","The artist’s popularity score on Spotify."],["followers","number","0+","Number of Spotify followers."],["genres","text list","",'Genre tags from Spotify (e.g. "indie rock", "electronic").'],["artist_gender","text","","Gender/vocal classification from MusicBrainz: male, female, mixed, instrumental, other, or unknown."],["artist_type","text","","MusicBrainz type: person, group, orchestra, choir, character, or other."],["artist_country","text","","Country of origin as an ISO code (e.g. US, GB, JP)."],["artist_area","text","","Area from MusicBrainz — may be a city, region, or country."],["artist_start_year","number","","The year the artist began activity."],["artist_end_year","number","","The year the artist ended activity. Empty if still active."],["is_active","boolean","true / false","Whether the artist is still active (no end date in MusicBrainz)."],["mb_genres","text list","","Genre tags from MusicBrainz for the artist."],["source_count","number","1+","How many times this artist appeared in the source stream (e.g. track count when using To Artists)."],["uri","text","","The Spotify artist URI."]]}},{id:"album-attributes",q:"What album attributes are available?",a:"When you use album sources (My Album Library, Album Entity, Album URIs) or the To Albums adapter, each album entity carries these attributes.",table:{headers:["Attribute","Type","Range","Description"],rows:[["name","text","","The album title."],["artist","text","","The primary artist name."],["popularity","number","0–100","The album’s popularity score on Spotify."],["release_date","date","","The album release date (YYYY-MM-DD)."],["label","text","","The record label."],["total_tracks","number","1+","Number of tracks on the album."],["album_type","text","","The album type: album, single, or compilation."],["source_count","number","1+","How many times this album appeared in the source stream (e.g. track count when using To Albums)."],["uri","text","","The Spotify album URI."]]}},{q:"How do I combine multiple playlists?",a:"Connect multiple sources to a combiner component. Use Alternate to interleave tracks from each source, Shuffler to randomly mix them, or MixIn to blend a secondary source into a primary one at a configurable ratio."},{q:"How does sharing work?",a:"Publishing a program creates a public link to the live version — anyone with the link can view the graph, and logged-in users can preview or import it. Edits you make will be visible to anyone who visits the link. When someone imports your program, they get a snapshot copy at that point in time — their copy is independent and won't change if you update yours. You can unshare at any time to revoke the link."},{q:"I used the old SmarterPlaylists — can I import my programs?",a:`Yes! If you had programs on the original SmarterPlaylists, you'll see an "Import" tab after logging in. Browse your legacy programs, preview them to see the dataflow graph, and selectively import the ones you want. Some old components (like EchoNest sources) are no longer available and will be marked as deprecated. Once you've imported everything you need, click "Done Importing" to hide the tab — you can always bring it back from the Programs page.`},{q:"Does SmarterPlaylists have dark mode?",a:"Yes! Click the theme icon in the header to cycle through three modes:",bullets:["System (monitor icon) — automatically follows your OS light/dark preference","Light (sun icon) — always light","Dark (moon icon) — always dark"]},{q:"Where can I ask more questions?",a:"There are a few places you can reach out:",links:[{text:"Music Machinery blog",href:"https://musicmachinery.com"},{text:"r/smarterplaylists on Reddit",href:"https://www.reddit.com/r/smarterplaylists/"},{text:"@plamere on Bluesky",href:"https://bsky.app/profile/plamere.bsky.social"}]},{q:"Who wrote this?",a:"SmarterPlaylists is built by Paul Lamere. Paul has been working at the intersection of music and technology for over 20 years and blogs about it at Music Machinery.",link:{text:"musicmachinery.com",href:"https://musicmachinery.com"}}]